From 871b5d5b8af3263034bd2a511c26641e6eae7db2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:18:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 165/182] integrator: Light Optimize, the render sampled as if
 the lights were brighter

The trick of multiplying every light and emission by k and dividing the
image by k afterwards changes the result only through Cycles' absolute
thresholds: the adaptive sampling criterion (error over the square root
of the intensity, so a k times brighter image looks sqrt(k) times
noisier and dark areas keep sampling) and the sample clamps. Use Light
Optimize / Light Optimize Factor (default 10) scale exactly those - the
intensity the criterion sees (KernelFilm.adaptive_exposure, in the film
padding) and the clamps - and leave the lights alone. A section of its
own in the Render Settings Cycles tab (regenerated; it also picks up
release/5.3's Filter Glossy default of 1).

Measured on a production frame, 960x640, 128 spp: same mean (0.01166 vs
0.01162), bright-area noise 0.113 -> 0.106, time +6 %. It only acts past
the adaptive minimum (64 samples at the default threshold).
